  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA surface cold front is positioned just northwest of the CWA\nthis afternoon across central Ohio, very gradually sliding\nsoutheastward towards the area. ISOLD showers and storms are \nbeginning to develop just ahead of and along the front where \nstronger low-level convergence exists, and also just south of \nthe CWA along the spine of the higher terrain via lift with \nanabatic convergence. Locally, weak mid-level capping across the\nforecast area has limited the region to mainly just fair \nweather Cu outside of an isolated shower. Synoptic lift should \ngradually increase throughout the late afternoon and evening as \nthe front slides into the forecast area, with ISOLD to SCT \nthunderstorm development expected given mixed-layer CAPE of \n1,000-2,000 J/kg and 0-6 km bulk shear of 20-30 kts. While the \noverall confidence in the severe threat for today has lowered \nsome, the potential for strong to isolated severe storms remains\nacross much of the CWA through this evening, with SPC \nmaintaining a Marginal Risk for severe thunderstorms across most\nof our forecast zones. Given a noted layer of mid-level dry air\nin progged soundings, the primary hazard continues to be \ndamaging winds with any stronger cores. While some hail cannot \nbe ruled out, freezing levels of ~ 13 kft will significantly \nlimit the potential for severe hail today.\n\nWhile a localized instance of flash flooding cannot be entirely\nruled out, the overall potential is low and significantly muted\ncompared to what has occurred earlier this month. For instance,\n12Z HREF neighborhood probabilities (within a 25 mile radius) \nof 1.5\" in an hour are by and large 10% or less area-wide \nthrough this evening. The chance for showers and storms will \nsteadily decrease shortly after sunset and become confined more \nso to in/near the higher terrain through early tonight, with dry\nweather area-wide by later tonight. Some river valley fog is \nexpected once again and could be dense in some locations. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nMainly dry weather returns to the area beginning Friday, with\njust the chance for ISOLD showers/storms across the higher\nterrain Friday and Saturday afternoon/evening via terrain \ninduced diurnal forcing (anabatic convergence). Dry conditions \nare then progged to persist through Tuesday as an upper ridge \nacross the central CONUS gradually shifts eastward overtop the \nforecast area. This will result in a gradual warming trend \nFriday onward, with near normal high temperatures on Friday \nrising to ~ 5 degrees above normal on Sunday onward, when upper \n80s to low 90s are expected across the lowlands, all amid plenty\nof sunshine. These hot temperatures will persist through at \nleast midweek. Warm overnight temperatures can be expected (mid \n60s to low 70s in the lowlands), amid at least some patchy river\nvalley fog potential each night given the time of year.",
